{
  "dom_id": "step-q274935",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n\nI. ${B}^{T}A⊙B\\,\\text{diag}(v)$\n\nII. $A⊙B{v}^{T}$\n\nIII. $AB⊙B{v}^{T}v$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*",
  "worked_solution": "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nFirst, notice that $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $v$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector.\n\nNow, let's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of ${B}^{T}A$ are $3\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $B\\,\\text{diag}(v)$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $A$ are $2\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $B{v}^{T}$ are $2\\times 1.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $AB$ are $2\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $B{v}^{T}v$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
